# University Physics Volume 1, chapters 14-17

§ 1 14.1
N S fluid | Fluid | ρ(r⃗), p(r⃗) |
d A liquid or gas: matter that flows because it cannot resist a shear stress when at rest; described by fields such as density and pressure.
m continuum: scalar fields ρ(r⃗, t), p(r⃗, t) on a region of ℝ³
g fluids
R[def] fluid <- stress, pres : at rest only normal stress (pressure), no shear stress | Matter that flows | Shear stress makes a fluid flow instead of deform elastically.
N O rho | Density | ρ = m/V |
d Mass per unit volume.
g density
R[def] rho <- m, L : ρ = m/V,  V ∼ ℓ³ | Mass per volume | For a nonuniform body ρ = dm/dV.
N O sg | Specific gravity | ρ/ρ_water |
d Density relative to that of water; dimensionless.
g specific gravity
R[def] sg <- rho : ρ/ρ_water | Ratio of densities | Above 1 the material sinks in water.
X pressure :: = pres

§ 1 14.2
N O pabs | Gauge and absolute pressure | p_abs = p_g + p_atm |
d Gauges read pressure relative to the atmosphere; absolute pressure adds atmospheric pressure.
g gauge pressure; absolute pressure
R[def] pabs <- pres : p_abs = p_g + p_atm | Relative to the atmosphere | Gauge pressure can be negative, absolute cannot.

§ 1 14.3
N M hydro | Hydrostatic equilibrium | dp/dy = −ρg |
d In a fluid at rest pressure grows with depth so that each fluid element is balanced: p = p₀ + ρgh for constant density.
p ρ constant ⇒ p linear in depth; pressure depends only on depth, not container shape; static
g hydrostatic equilibrium
R[thm] hydro <- n2, rho, g, pres, fluid : (p + dp)A − pA = −ρgA dy | Forces on a fluid layer | Net force on a thin layer is zero.
N M pascal | Pascal's principle | Δp transmitted undiminished |
d A pressure change applied to an enclosed fluid reaches every part of it; a hydraulic jack multiplies force by the area ratio.
p consequence of hydrostatic equilibrium; F₁/A₁ = F₂/A₂ in hydraulics; work is still conserved
g Pascal’s principle; hydraulic jack
R[thm] pascal <- hydro : p(y) = p₀ + ρg(y₀ − y);  Δp₀ shifts p everywhere | Additive constant | The pressure profile is fixed up to the pressure at one point.

§ 1 14.4
N O FB | Buoyant force | F_B = ρ_fl V g |
d Net upward force of a fluid on an immersed body.
g buoyant force

N M archi | Archimedes' principle | F_B = weight of displaced fluid |
d A body immersed in a fluid is pushed up by a force equal to the weight of the fluid it displaces.
p consequence of hydrostatic pressure; independent of the body's material; body floats if its mean density < fluid density
g Archimedes’ principle
R[thm] archi <- hydro, w : F⃗_B = −∮ p n̂ dA = weight of the displaced fluid | Pressure difference top to bottom | The same pressure would hold up the fluid that fills the space.
R[thm] FB <- archi, rho, g : F_B = ρ_fluid V_displaced g | Weight of displaced fluid | Uses the fluid's density, not the body's.
§ 1 14.5
N M ideal | Ideal fluid | incompressible, nonviscous |
d A model fluid with constant density and no internal friction; its steady flow is laminar.
p ρ constant; no viscosity; flow steady and laminar
g ideal fluid
R[approx] ideal <- fluid : ρ = const,  η = 0 | Idealisation | Neglects compressibility and viscosity.
N O eta | Viscosity | η = FL/(vA) |
d Internal friction of a fluid: the shear stress needed per velocity gradient.
g viscosity
R[def] eta <- stress, speed : η = (F/A)/(v/L) | Shear stress per velocity gradient | Measured by shearing a fluid layer between plates.
N S flow | Fluid flow | v⃗(r⃗, t) |
d The motion of a fluid: a velocity at every point. In laminar flow the layers slide smoothly; in turbulent flow eddies mix them.
m vector field v⃗(r⃗, t) on the fluid region; streamlines tangent to v⃗
g laminar flow; turbulent flow
R[def] flow <- fluid, vvec : v⃗(r⃗, t) | Velocity field | Each fluid element has a velocity.
N O Q | Flow rate | Q = dV/dt = Av |
d Volume of fluid passing a cross section per unit time.
g flow rate
R[def] Q <- flow : Q = dV/dt = A v̄ | Volume per time | Area times mean speed.
N M contin | Continuity equation | ρ₁A₁v₁ = ρ₂A₂v₂ |
d Mass entering a flow tube equals mass leaving; for an incompressible fluid A₁v₁ = A₂v₂, so the flow speeds up where the tube narrows.
p local mass conservation; Av constant for incompressible flow; steady flow assumed
R[thm] contin <- Q, rho, flow : ρAv constant along a flow tube | Mass conservation | No mass is created or stored in steady flow.

§ 1 14.6
N M bern | Bernoulli's equation | p + ½ρv² + ρgy = const |
d Along a streamline of an ideal fluid, pressure plus kinetic and potential energy per volume is constant; faster flow means lower pressure (Bernoulli's principle).
p work–energy theorem per volume; valid for steady, incompressible, nonviscous flow; along a streamline
g Bernoulli’s equation; Bernoulli’s principle
R[thm] bern <- wet, ideal, contin, pres : W_pressure = (p₁ − p₂)ΔV = Δ(½ρv² + ρgy)ΔV | Work–energy for a fluid parcel | Pressure does the work that changes kinetic and potential energy.

§ 1 14.7
X turbulence :: = flow
N M poise | Poiseuille's law | Q = (p₂ − p₁)πr⁴/(8ηl) |
d Laminar viscous flow through a tube: flow rate is the pressure difference over a resistance R = 8ηl/(πr⁴).
p laminar flow only; Q ∝ r⁴; resistance analogous to an electrical resistance
g Poiseuille’s law; Poiseuille’s law for resistance
R[thm] poise <- eta, flow, Q, pres : Q = Δp/R,  R = 8ηl/(πr⁴) | Viscous flow in a tube | Parabolic velocity profile integrated over the cross section.
N O Re | Reynolds number | N_R = 2ρvr/η |
d Dimensionless ratio of inertial to viscous effects; flow in a tube is laminar below about 2000 and turbulent above about 3000.
g Reynolds number
R[def] Re <- rho, speed, eta : N_R = 2ρvr/η | Inertia over viscosity | Predicts the onset of turbulence.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Chapter 15
§ 1 15.1
N O Tper | Period | T |
d Time for one cycle of a periodic motion (an oscillation).
g periodic motion; period (T); oscillation
R[def] Tper <- dtm : T = Δt per cycle | Duration of a cycle | The motion repeats after T.
N O freq | Frequency | f = 1/T |
d Number of cycles per unit time; SI unit the hertz.
g frequency (f)
R[def] freq <- Tper : f = 1/T | Inverse of the period | Cycles per second.
N M sho | Simple harmonic oscillator | m d²x/dt² = −kx |
d A mass on an ideal spring: the restoring force is proportional to displacement, giving simple harmonic motion.
p linear second-order equation; ω = √(k/m) independent of amplitude (isochronous); solutions superpose
g simple harmonic oscillator; simple harmonic motion (SHM)
R[law] sho <- n2, hooke : m ẍ = −k x | Second law with a Hooke force | The equation of motion of a spring–mass system.
N S shm | Harmonic oscillation | x(t) = A cos(ωt + φ) |
d The oscillator's state: amplitude A and phase shift φ about the equilibrium position; equivalently the pair (x, v) at one instant.
m (A, φ) ∈ [0, ∞) × S¹; equivalently a point (x, v) on an ellipse in the phase plane
g amplitude (A); equilibrium position; phase shift
R[thm] shm <- sho : x(t) = A cos(ωt + φ) | Solution of the oscillator equation | A and φ are fixed by the initial position and velocity.
N O kspr | Force constant | k |
d Stiffness of a spring: force per unit stretch, in N/m.
g force constant (k)
R[def] kspr <- hooke : k = −F/x | Slope of the force law | Measured from the spring's response.
R[thm] omega <- sho, kspr, m : ω = √(k/m) = 2πf | Oscillator frequency | The angular frequency reappears as the oscillator's natural frequency.
R[thm] Tper <- omega : T = 2π/ω = 2π√(m/k) | Period of the oscillator | Independent of amplitude.

§ 1 15.2
X elastic potential energy :: = Uel
N O Frest | Restoring force | F = −k(x − x₀) |
d Force that pushes a body back toward a stable equilibrium point; linear for small displacements.
g restoring force
R[thm] Frest <- eqpt, F, U : F ≈ −U″(x₀)(x − x₀) | Near a minimum of U | Every stable equilibrium behaves like a spring for small displacements.
X stable equilibrium point :: = eqpt
R[thm] Emech <- shm, Uel, K : E = ½kx² + ½mv² = ½kA² | Energy of the oscillator | Kinetic and potential energy exchange, their sum fixed by the amplitude.

§ 1 15.3
R[analog] shm <- circ, omega : x(t) = A cos(ωt + φ) is the projection of uniform circular motion | Reference circle | Simple harmonic motion is one component of uniform circular motion.

§ 1 15.4
N M spend | Simple pendulum | T = 2π√(L/g) |
d A point mass on a light string; for small angles it is a harmonic oscillator whose period depends only on length and g.
p small-angle approximation sin θ ≈ θ; period independent of mass and (small) amplitude
g simple pendulum
R[approx] spend <- sho, n2, g : d²θ/dt² = −(g/L) sin θ ≈ −(g/L)θ | Small angles | The same equation as the spring with k/m → g/L.
N M ppend | Physical pendulum | T = 2π√(I/(mgL)) |
d An extended body swinging about a pivot; its period depends on its moment of inertia and the distance L from pivot to centre of mass.
p small angles; reduces to the simple pendulum for I = mL²
g physical pendulum
R[approx] ppend <- n2rot, I, sho, w : I d²θ/dt² = −mgL sin θ ≈ −mgLθ | Torque of gravity | Second law for rotation with small angles.
N M tpend | Torsional pendulum | T = 2π√(I/κ) |
d A body twisting on a wire whose restoring torque is proportional to the twist angle.
p linear restoring torque τ = −κθ; exact harmonic motion for elastic twist
g torsional pendulum
R[thm] tpend <- n2rot, I, sho : I d²θ/dt² = −κθ | Restoring torque | Rotational version of the spring–mass system.

§ 1 15.5
N M damp | Damped oscillator | mẍ + bẋ + kx = 0 |
d An oscillator with a drag force proportional to velocity; it is underdamped (decaying oscillation), critically damped or overdamped (no oscillation).
p linear; underdamped b² < 4mk: x = A₀e^{−bt/2m} cos(ωt + φ), ω = √(ω₀² − (b/2m)²); critical b² = 4mk; overdamped b² > 4mk
g natural angular frequency; underdamped; critically damped; overdamped
R[gen] damp <- sho, FD : add −b v (slow viscous drag) | Oscillator with drag | The linear drag law added to the spring force.

§ 1 15.6
N M forced | Driven oscillator and resonance | mẍ + bẋ + kx = F₀ sin ωt |
d A damped oscillator pushed periodically settles into motion at the driving frequency; its amplitude peaks (resonance) when the driving frequency is near the natural one.
p steady state at the driving frequency; A = F₀/√(m²(ω² − ω₀²)² + b²ω²); sharper resonance for weaker damping
g resonance
R[gen] forced <- damp : add a periodic driving force F₀ sin ωt | Driven damped oscillator | The response is largest near ω = ω₀.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Chapter 16
§ 1 16.1
N S wave | Wave | y(x, t) |
d A disturbance that travels through a medium (mechanical wave) while each part of the medium only oscillates about its equilibrium; transverse or longitudinal.
m displacement field y(x, t) of the medium, ℝ × ℝ → ℝ (or a vector field in 3D)
g wave; mechanical wave; transverse wave; longitudinal wave
R[gen] wave <- shm, sys : coupled oscillators of a medium, each delayed along the propagation | Oscillation that travels | Neighbouring parts of the medium push on each other.
N O lam | Wavelength | λ |
d Distance between adjacent identical points of a periodic wave.
g wavelength
R[read] lam <- wave : y(x + λ, t) = y(x, t) | Spatial period | Read off a snapshot of the wave.
N O vwave | Wave speed | v = λ/T = λf |
d Speed at which the disturbance propagates; set by the medium, not by the source.
g wave velocity; wave speed
R[thm] vwave <- lam, Tper, freq : v = λ/T = λ f | One wavelength per period | The pattern advances λ during each period.

§ 1 16.2
N S trav | Traveling wave function | y = f(x ∓ vt) |
d A wave shape that moves without changing; a pulse is a single such disturbance and y = A sin(kx − ωt + φ) is the periodic case.
m function of the single variable x − vt (or x + vt); periodic case y = A sin(kx ∓ ωt + φ)
g wave function; pulse
R[thm] trav <- wave, vwave : y(x, t) = f(x − vt) | Shape moving at v | The same profile shifted by vt.
N O kwave | Wave number | k = 2π/λ |
d Spatial angular frequency of a wave, in rad/m; v = ω/k.
g wave number
R[def] kwave <- lam : k = 2π/λ | Radians per length | Companion of ω = 2π/T.
N M waveeq | Linear wave equation | ∂²y/∂x² = (1/v²) ∂²y/∂t² |
d The equation satisfied by every wave travelling at speed v in a linear medium.
p linear (solutions superpose); second order in x and t; general solution f(x − vt) + g(x + vt)
g linear wave equation
R[thm] waveeq <- trav, ddt : ∂²f(x − vt)/∂t² = v² ∂²f(x − vt)/∂x² | Differentiate the wave function | Every travelling profile satisfies it.

§ 1 16.3
R[thm] waveeq <- n2, T, lmd : μ dx ∂²y/∂t² = F_T ∂²y/∂x² dx | Second law for a string element | The net transverse tension force on a small piece of string gives the wave equation.
R[thm] vwave <- T, lmd, waveeq : v = √(F_T/μ) | Speed on a string | Tension over linear mass density.
R[thm] vwave <- Bmod, rho : v = √(B/ρ) | Compression wave in a fluid | Elastic property over inertial property.

§ 1 16.4
N O Pwave | Wave power | P_ave = ½μA²ω²v |
d Energy per unit time carried by a wave; proportional to the square of the amplitude and of the frequency.
R[thm] Pwave <- trav, lmd, omega, vwave, K : P_ave = ½ μ A² ω² v | Energy per wavelength per period | Kinetic plus potential energy of the string per wavelength, divided by T.
N O Iint | Intensity | I = P/A |
d Power per unit area carried by a wave; for a spherical wave I = P/(4πr²).
g intensity (I)
R[def] Iint <- Pwave : I = P/A;  spherical I = P/(4πr²) | Power per area | Falls as 1/r² for a point source.

§ 1 16.5
N M bc | Boundary conditions | fixed or free end |
d At a fixed end the displacement is zero and a reflected pulse is inverted; at a free end the slope is zero and the pulse returns upright.
p fixed: y = 0; free: ∂y/∂x = 0; partial reflection and transmission at a change of medium
g fixed boundary condition; free boundary condition
R[thm] bc <- waveeq : reflected wave chosen to satisfy the end condition | Reflection | The incident and reflected waves together meet the condition at the end.
N S interf | Interference | y_R = [2A cos(φ/2)] sin(kx − ωt + φ/2) |
d Two overlapping waves add constructively (in phase, double amplitude) or destructively (half a cycle apart, cancel).
m superposed wave field; resultant amplitude 2A|cos(φ/2)| set by the phase difference φ
g interference; constructive interference; destructive interference

N M superp | Superposition principle | y = y₁ + y₂ |
d When waves overlap, the displacements add; follows from the linearity of the wave equation.
p linearity; holds for small amplitudes in linear media; waves pass through each other unchanged
g superposition
R[thm] superp <- waveeq : L[y₁ + y₂] = L[y₁] + L[y₂] = 0 | Linearity | Sums of solutions are solutions.
R[thm] interf <- superp, trav : A sin(kx − ωt) + A sin(kx − ωt + φ) | Sum of two waves | Trigonometric identity for the sum of two sines.
§ 1 16.6
N S standw | Standing wave | y = [2A sin kx] cos ωt |
d Two equal waves moving in opposite directions produce a pattern that does not travel: nodes stay at rest, antinodes oscillate most.
m product of a fixed spatial profile and a time oscillation; nodes at kx = nπ
g standing wave; node; antinode
R[thm] standw <- superp, trav, bc : A sin(kx − ωt) + A sin(kx + ωt) = 2A sin kx cos ωt | Counter-propagating waves | Reflection from the ends supplies the second wave.
N S modes | Normal modes | λ_n = 2L/n,  f_n = n v/2L |
d A string fixed at both ends can only hold standing waves that fit its length: the fundamental and its overtones.
m discrete family n = 1, 2, 3, …; any motion is a superposition of modes
g normal mode; fundamental frequency; overtone
R[thm] modes <- standw, bc, vwave : nodes at both ends ⇒ L = nλ/2 | Fit the length | The boundary conditions select the wavelengths.
R[thm] forced <- modes : driving at f_n gives large amplitude | Resonance of a string | Each normal mode is an oscillator that resonates when driven at its frequency.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Chapter 17
§ 1 17.1
N S sound | Sound wave | Δp(x, t),  s(x, t) |
d A longitudinal wave of compressions and rarefactions in a medium; the pressure wave is a quarter cycle out of phase with the displacement wave.
m pressure field Δp = Δp_max sin(kx ∓ ωt + φ) and displacement field s = s_max cos(kx ∓ ωt + φ)
g sound
R[thm] sound <- wave, pres, Bmod : Δp = −B ∂s/∂x | Pressure wave in a fluid | The displacement wave compresses and expands the medium.
X hearing :: perception of sound (physiology)

§ 1 17.2
N O vsound | Speed of sound | v = √(B/ρ) |
d Set by an elastic property over an inertial property of the medium: √(B/ρ) in fluids, √(Y/ρ) in solids, √(γRT/M) in an ideal gas.
R[thm] vsound <- sound, Bmod, rho : v = √(B/ρ) | Elastic over inertial property | Newton's second law for a slab of fluid.
R[thm] vsound <- Ymod, rho : v = √(Y/ρ) | In a solid rod | Young's modulus replaces B.

§ 1 17.3
N O beta | Sound intensity level | β = 10 log₁₀(I/I₀) dB |
d Logarithmic measure of intensity relative to the threshold I₀ = 10⁻¹² W/m².
g sound intensity level
R[def] beta <- Iint : β = 10 log₁₀(I/I₀) | Logarithmic scale | A factor 10 in intensity adds 10 dB.
R[thm] Iint <- sound, rho, vsound : I = (Δp_max)²/(2ρv) | Intensity of a sound wave | From the pressure amplitude.
X sound pressure level; loudness; phon; pitch; notes; timbre :: how sound is perceived and described in music; no new model
X transducer :: a device

§ 1 17.4
N S tubemodes | Air-column modes | f_n = nv/2L or nv/4L |
d Standing sound waves in a tube: open at both ends all harmonics f_n = nv/2L; closed at one end only odd ones, f_n = nv/4L.
m discrete modes; displacement antinode at an open end, node at a closed end
g fundamental; harmonics; overtones
R[thm] tubemodes <- modes, sound, bc : open end ↔ antinode, closed end ↔ node | Boundary conditions in a tube | The string's mode counting with acoustic end conditions.

§ 1 17.6
N O fbeat | Beat frequency | f_B = |f₂ − f₁| |
d Two sounds of slightly different frequency produce a loudness that rises and falls at the difference frequency.
g beats; beat frequency
R[thm] fbeat <- superp, freq : cos ω₁t + cos ω₂t = 2 cos(Δω t/2) cos(ω̄t) | Superposition in time | Slowly varying envelope at the difference frequency.

§ 1 17.7
N O fdop | Doppler-shifted frequency | f_o = f_s (v ± v_o)/(v ∓ v_s) |
d The frequency heard changes when source or observer moves through the medium: higher when approaching, lower when receding.
g Doppler effect; Doppler shift
R[thm] fdop <- vsound, freq, galrel : wavelength compressed by the source's motion, crossing rate changed by the observer's | Moving source and observer | Velocities are measured relative to the medium.

§ 1 17.8
N S shock | Shock wave | sin θ = v/v_s = 1/M |
d A source faster than sound piles up its wavefronts on a cone; the pressure jump is heard as a sonic boom, and a boat's bow wake is the surface analogue.
m conical envelope of spherical wavefronts, half-angle θ with sin θ = v/v_s (Mach number M = v_s/v)
g shock wave; sonic boom; bow wake
R[thm] shock <- vsound, superp : wavefronts emitted at earlier positions overlap on a cone | Supersonic source | Constructive superposition along the envelope.
